<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Syrmia</h1><p class="st-meta">1282 CE – 1316 CE · capital Debrc</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Realm of the former Serbian king Stefan Dragutin on the Sava and Danube, held as a Hungarian fief and rival to his brother's Serbia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1282 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dragutin">Stefan Dragutin</a>, king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a>, gave up the throne to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Milutin</a> after a riding accident, keeping some Serbian lands. Through his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-hungary-queen-of-serbia">Catherine</a>, daughter of Stephen V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, he received from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-iv-of-hungary">Ladislaus IV</a> in 1284 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banate-of-macso">Banate of Macsó</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usora">Usora</a> and Soli.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dragutin">Dragutin</a>, styled king, ruled from Debrc near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, supporting the Catholic Church in his lands as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monasteries. He joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Milutin</a> against the Bulgarian lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/branicevo">Braničevo</a>, but from about 1301 the brothers fought for years over the Serbian succession.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dragutin">Dragutin</a>'s death in 1316 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Milutin</a> seized most of his lands, imprisoning Dragutin's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladislav-ii-of-serbia">Vladislav II</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> soon retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macva">Mačva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, restoring the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banate-of-macso">Banate of Macsó</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-syrmia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Syrmia" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Polserb · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dragutin-syrmia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1282 CE – 1316 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Debrc</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stefan Dragutin, Vladislav II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity; Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dragutin's Kingdom, Kingdom of Srem</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1299&amp;focus=kingdom-of-syrmia" aria-label="Kingdom of Syrmia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5155 600 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Syrmia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5475 856L5521 939M5475 856L5446 783"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5521 939h0M5446 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5475 856h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5475 856h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Debrc. <a href="/atlas?year=1299&amp;focus=kingdom-of-syrmia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
